* 14. SHELL SCRIPTING FUNDAMENTALS
** Core Concepts
   - Shebang Line     :: =#!/bin/bash= (First file line; tells system which interpreter to execute script)
   - Script Execution :: Make executable using =chmod +x script.sh=, then initiate with =./script.sh=
** Input & Read Management
   - read name                  :: Accept standard keyboard string input into variable '$name'
   - read -p "Enter Age: " age  :: Prompt interactive user with string message during input sequence
   - read n1 n2 n3              :: Gather multiple distinct data variables in sequence via single step
** Variables Manipulation
   - Rules :: No spaces allowed around assignments (e.g., =msg="Hello"= is valid; =msg = "Hello"= fails)
   - Reference Usage :: Access variable values by prefixing string with dollar symbol identifier: =$msg=
** Command Substitution
   Capture execution outputs directly inside functional operational parameters.
   - Example 1 :: =date_=$(date)=
   - Example 2 :: =lines=$(wc -l < file.txt)=
** Arithmetic Actions
   Perform math parameters using structural =$(( ))= wrapping syntax wrappers. No internal spacing required.
   - Example :: =sum=$((a + b))=
** Positional & Command Arguments
   - $0 :: File name identity of execution script
   - $1 :: First argument input parameter fed to script engine
   - $2 :: Second argument input parameter fed to script engine
   - $# :: Integer metric totaling number of arguments supplied to script interface
   - $@ :: Array string mapping capturing all argument parameters supplied to script interface
   - shift :: Move positional arguments leftward by one structural index slot ($2 becomes $1)
** Script Debugging & Safety Directives
   - set -e        :: Halt execution sequence instantly if any script command reports error failures
   - set -u        :: Treat unmapped unassigned variables as explicit structural code errors
   - set -o pipefail :: Intercept and report errors tracking mid-stream inside pipe connections
   - trap 'echo error' ERR :: Event listener wrapper to trigger alert routines during error faults
   - $?            :: Output exit check status state flag monitored from the last executed statement
** Conditionals Syntax Block
   Always retain isolated spaces inside evaluation square brackets context layout structures: =[ ]=.
   - Evaluators :: =-eq= (equal), =-ne= (not equal), =-gt= (greater than), =-lt= (less than)
   - String Flags :: === (equal strings), =!= (not equal strings)
#+BEGIN_SRC bash
if [ "$1" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "Zero"
elif [ "$1" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "Positive"
else
    echo "Negative"
fi
#+END_SRC
